\begin{abstract}
We study the \textbf{EML} (Exponential--Multiplicative--Logarithmic) function class --- the
closure of the elementary algebraic operations together with the transcendental primitives
$\exp$ and $\log$ --- as a vehicle for universal approximation, and we establish two
complementary results. First, a \emph{qualitative} density theorem: on the unit cube $[0,1]^n$,
the subalgebra generated by the $n$ coordinate exponential features
$\{x \mapsto e^{x_i}\}_{i=1}^n$ is uniformly dense in the space $C([0,1]^n,\R)$ of real
continuous functions. Equivalently, every continuous function on the cube is the uniform limit
of exponential polynomials in the coordinates, and for every $\varepsilon>0$ an explicit
$\varepsilon$-approximant exists. The proof is a family-level application of the
Stone--Weierstrass theorem, in which point separation is provided by the injectivity of a
\emph{single} nonlinearity, $\exp$, while the dimension forces the \emph{width} (one feature per
coordinate). On the cube we further record the quantitative shallow-feature range bound
$1 \le e^{x_i} \le e$, the explicit constant that an abstract density theorem on a general
compact set cannot express. Second, a \emph{quantitative} depth result: the monomial
$x \mapsto x^n$ admits a naive multiplication-only EML representation of depth $n$ and an
exp/log representation $\exp(n\ln x)$ of constant depth $3$, both exact on $(0,\infty)$; for all
$n \ge 4$ the exp/log form is strictly shallower, and the depth gap $n-3$ grows without bound.
This is a precise separation showing that $\exp$ and $\log$ compress unbounded multiplicative
depth to a constant. All results have been formally verified.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} universal approximation, Stone--Weierstrass theorem, exponential
polynomial, coordinate exponential features, unit cube, density, depth compression, monomial,
EML class.

\section{Introduction}

Universal approximation theorems are the theoretical foundation of function-approximation
methods across machine learning, numerical analysis, and surrogate modeling. The canonical
statement asserts that some structured family of simple functions is \emph{dense} in a target
space of continuous functions: any continuous target can be approximated to arbitrary uniform
accuracy by a member of the family. Classical instances include polynomials (Weierstrass),
shallow neural networks with non-polynomial activations, and abstractly, any point-separating
subalgebra (Stone--Weierstrass).

This paper concerns the \textbf{EML class}: functions built from the field operations of $\R$
together with the two transcendental primitives $\exp$ and $\log$. Two questions organize the
study.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Expressivity (density).} \emph{Which} functions does an EML class reach? We answer
this on the canonical domain of approximation theory, the unit cube $[0,1]^n$, for the minimal
feature set consisting of the coordinate exponentials $x \mapsto e^{x_i}$.
\item \textbf{Cost (depth).} \emph{How expensive} is an exact EML representation, measured in
compositional depth? We answer this for the simplest nontrivial target family, the monomials
$x^n$, and exhibit an unbounded separation between multiplication-only depth and exp/log depth.
\end{enumerate}

Density is a soft, qualitative property: it follows from point separation alone and is
indifferent to the geometry of the (compact) domain. Depth is a hard, quantitative property:
it depends on the multiplicative complexity of the target, and it is exactly here that $\exp$
and $\log$ become indispensable. Throughout, we treat $\exp$ as the source of nonlinearity and
the pair $(\exp,\log)$ as a depth-reducing lever.

\section{Preliminaries and definitions}

\subsection{The unit cube}

\begin{definition}[unit cube]
For $n \in \N$ the \emph{unit cube} is
\[
[0,1]^n = \{\, x \in \R^n : 0 \le x_i \le 1 \text{ for all } i \,\} = \prod_{i=1}^n [0,1].
\]
Formally we model $\R^n$ as the function space $\mathrm{Fin}\,n \to \R$ and set
$[0,1]^n = \{x : \forall i,\ x_i \in [0,1]\}$, an indexed product of the closed interval.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[compactness]
The unit cube $[0,1]^n$ is compact.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The closed interval $[0,1]$ is compact (Heine--Borel). A finite product of compact spaces is
compact (Tychonoff), so $\prod_i [0,1]$ is compact; as a subtype it carries the structure of a
compact topological space.
\end{proof}

Compactness is essential: uniform approximation of continuous functions, and the
Stone--Weierstrass theorem itself, require a compact domain.

\subsection{The space of continuous functions}

We write $C(K,\R)$ for the $\R$-algebra of continuous real-valued functions on a compact set
$K$, with the uniform norm $\|f\| = \sup_{x\in K}|f(x)|$. Convergence in this norm is uniform
convergence; the algebra operations are pointwise.

\subsection{Coordinate exponential features and the EML subalgebra}

\begin{definition}[coordinate feature]
For $K \subseteq \R^n$ compact and an index $i$, the \emph{$i$-th coordinate feature} is the
continuous map $\coord_i : K \to \R$, $\coord_i(x) = x_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[coordinate exponential family]
The \emph{coordinate exponential family} is
\[
\mathcal{E}_K = \{\, \exp \circ \coord_i : i = 1,\ldots,n \,\}
= \{\, x \mapsto e^{x_i} : i = 1,\ldots,n \,\} \subseteq C(K,\R).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[EML subalgebra]
The \emph{coordinate-exponential EML subalgebra} is
$\Adj(\mathcal{E}_K) := \mathrm{adjoin}_{\R}(\mathcal{E}_K)$, the smallest unital
$\R$-subalgebra of $C(K,\R)$ containing $\mathcal{E}_K$. Since $e^a e^b = e^{a+b}$, every
element is an \emph{exponential polynomial in the coordinates},
\[
p(x) = \sum_{k \in S} c_k\, e^{k_1 x_1 + \cdots + k_n x_n}, \qquad
S \subseteq \N^n \text{ finite},\ c_k \in \R,
\]
the single-hidden-layer exponential network with integer weights on the coordinate
exponentials.
\end{definition}

\subsection{EML terms and depth}

An \textbf{EML term} is a finite expression tree built from a variable $x$, real constants, and
the operations multiplication, $\exp$, and $\log$. Each term $t$ carries an \emph{evaluation}
$\eval(t)$ (interpreting the operations on $(0,\infty)$) and a \emph{depth} $\dpth(t) \in \N$,
the height of the tree, i.e.\ the maximum number of nested operations on a root-to-leaf path.

\begin{definition}[naive monomial term]
The \emph{naive} monomial term is the right-nested product of $n$ copies of the variable,
$\monoNaive(n) = \underbrace{x \cdot (x \cdot (\cdots \cdot x))}_{n}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[exp/log monomial term]
The \emph{exp/log} monomial term is $\monoExpLog(n) = \exp(n \cdot \log x)$, the composition of
$\log$, scalar multiplication by the constant $n$, and $\exp$.
\end{definition}

\section{Main results: density on the cube}

\begin{lemma}[separation]\label{lem:sep}
The coordinate exponential family $\mathcal{E}_K$ separates the points of any
$K \subseteq \R^n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $x \ne y$ in $K$. Being distinct vectors, they differ in some coordinate: $x_i \ne y_i$ for
some $i$. The exponential is strictly monotone, hence injective, so $e^{x_i} \ne e^{y_i}$. The
feature $\exp \circ \coord_i \in \mathcal{E}_K$ thus separates $x$ and $y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[EML density on the unit cube, \texttt{eml\_dense\_unitCube}]\label{thm:dense}
The topological closure of the coordinate-exponential EML subalgebra on the unit cube is the
whole continuous-function algebra:
\[
\overline{\mathrm{adjoin}_{\R}\big(\mathcal{E}_{[0,1]^n}\big)} = C\big([0,1]^n,\R\big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply the Stone--Weierstrass theorem in its subalgebra form. The cube is compact, so the
theorem applies in $C([0,1]^n,\R)$. The subalgebra $\Adj(\mathcal{E}_{[0,1]^n})$ is unital and,
by Lemma~\ref{lem:sep}, generated by a point-separating family, hence itself point-separating.
Stone--Weierstrass gives that its uniform closure is all of $C([0,1]^n,\R)$. Concretely the
result instantiates the general compact-domain density theorem at $K=[0,1]^n$, the only added
ingredient being compactness of the cube.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\varepsilon$-approximation, \texttt{eml\_approx\_unitCube}]
For every $f \in C([0,1]^n,\R)$ and every $\varepsilon > 0$ there exists an exponential
polynomial $p \in \mathrm{adjoin}_{\R}(\mathcal{E}_{[0,1]^n})$ with
\[
\|p - f\| = \sup_{x \in [0,1]^n} |p(x) - f(x)| < \varepsilon.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Operational restatement of Theorem~\ref{thm:dense}: membership of $f$ in the uniform closure of
the subalgebra is exactly the existence of a subalgebra element within any prescribed uniform
distance $\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Width necessity (sharpness)}
A single \emph{ridge} feature $x \mapsto g(w_1 x_1 + \cdots + w_n x_n)$ has separating power
bounded by the linear functional $x \mapsto \sum_i w_i x_i$. A linear map $\R^n \to \R$ cannot
be injective for $n \ge 2$, since injectivity would force $n = \dim \R^n \le \dim \R = 1$. Hence
no single ridge feature separates points in dimension $\ge 2$, and at least $n$ coordinate
features are required: width is forced by dimension while nonlinearity is supplied by a single
$\exp$.

\begin{theorem}[shallow-feature range bound, \texttt{coordExp\_bounds\_unitCube}]\label{thm:bound}
For every $x \in [0,1]^n$ and every coordinate $i$, $\ 1 \le e^{x_i} \le e$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
From $0 \le x_i \le 1$ and monotonicity of $\exp$, $\ e^0 \le e^{x_i} \le e^1$, with $e^0 = 1$,
$e^1 = e$.
\end{proof}

This is the quantitative datum that the abstract density theorem cannot express: it confines each
single-layer feature to $[1,e]$, controlling the modulus of continuity of the shallow layer.

\section{Main results: depth compression of monomials}

Fix the domain $(0,\infty)$ so that $\log$ is defined and $x^n = e^{n\ln x}$.

\begin{proposition}[naive monomial, \texttt{monoNaive\_eval}, \texttt{monoNaive\_depth}]
For all $x > 0$, $\ \eval(\monoNaive(n))(x) = x^n$, and $\ \dpth(\monoNaive(n)) = n$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By induction on $n$: the right-nested product of $n$ copies of $x$ multiplies to $x^n$ and has
tree height $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[exp/log monomial, \texttt{monoExpLog\_eval}, \texttt{monoExpLog\_depth}]
For all $x > 0$, $\ \eval(\monoExpLog(n))(x) = x^n$, and $\ \dpth(\monoExpLog(n)) = 3$ for every
$n$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $x>0$, $\exp(n \ln x) = \exp(\ln(x^n)) = x^n$ by $\exp\circ\ln = \mathrm{id}$ on
$(0,\infty)$ and $\ln(x^n) = n\ln x$. The term is a fixed three-level tree
($\log$, multiply-by-constant, $\exp$) whose height is independent of $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[depth compression, \texttt{eml\_depth\_compression}]\label{thm:comp}
For every degree $n \ge 4$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item for all $x > 0$, $\ \eval(\monoNaive(n))(x) = \eval(\monoExpLog(n))(x) = x^n$;
\item $\dpth(\monoExpLog(n)) = 3 < n = \dpth(\monoNaive(n))$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Part (1) is the two propositions above. Part (2) substitutes $3$ and $n$ and uses
$n \ge 4 > 3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[unbounded depth gap, \texttt{eml\_depth\_unbounded\_gap}]
The depth gap is
$\ \dpth(\monoNaive(n)) - \dpth(\monoExpLog(n)) = n - 3$, and
$\ \lim_{n\to\infty}(n-3) = \infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The gap is $n-3$ by the two propositions; the affine map $n \mapsto n-3$ diverges.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Interpretation}
A fixed depth-$3$ EML circuit represents monomials of arbitrarily high degree exactly, while the
multiplication-only construction grows linearly in depth. The lever is $x^n = e^{n\ln x}$,
which converts $n-1$ nested multiplications into one scalar multiply between a single $\log$ and
a single $\exp$. This is the formal sense in which representation cost depends on depth, and the
structural justification for treating $\exp$ and $\log$ as primitive EML operations.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Exponential-polynomial approximation on the cube.}
Given samples of a continuous target $f$ on a grid over $[0,1]^n$, build the design matrix of
features $\Phi_{x,k} = e^{\langle k, x\rangle} = \prod_i e^{k_i x_i}$ for a finite multi-index
set $S$, and solve $\min_c \|\Phi c - f\|_2$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:dense} the uniform error tends
to $0$ as $S$ is enriched; columns are numerically benign because Theorem~\ref{thm:bound}
confines each base feature to $[1,e]$.

\paragraph{Depth-aware monomial evaluation.}
To evaluate $x^n$, either fold $n$ multiplications (depth $n$) or compute $\exp(n\log x)$
(depth $3$); Theorem~\ref{thm:comp} guarantees both equal $x^n$ on $(0,\infty)$.

\section{Applications}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Universal approximation with explicit primitives.} Theorem~\ref{thm:dense}
furnishes a closed-form universal family on $[0,1]^n$ where every approximant is an exponential
polynomial that can be written down and differentiated symbolically.
\item \textbf{Conditioning and feature scaling.} Theorem~\ref{thm:bound}
($e^{x_i}\in[1,e]$) gives an a priori bound on feature magnitudes, useful for numerical
conditioning and data-independent normalization.
\item \textbf{Architecture economy.} The unbounded depth gap is a provable instance of the
depth/nonlinearity tradeoff: the right transcendental primitives buy unbounded depth savings on
structured targets, as hardware power routines ($a^b = e^{b\ln a}$) already exploit.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion and future directions}

The cycle establishes two complementary facts: density is qualitative and domain-agnostic among
compacta, while depth is governed by multiplicative complexity and collapsed by $\exp$/$\log$.

\begin{conjecture}[depth lower bound for multiplication-only terms]
Every EML term in the $\mathrm{mul}$-only fragment (no $\exp$/$\log$) that evaluates to $x^n$ on
$(0,\infty)$ has depth $\ge \lceil \log_2 n \rceil$, attained by the balanced binary product.
Hence multiplication-only minimal depth is $\Theta(\log n)$ --- still unbounded, so exp/log
compression to constant depth is genuine.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[quantitative depth/width law on the cube]
On $[0,1]^n$, approximating an $L$-Lipschitz target to uniform error $\varepsilon$ with a
coordinate-exponential EML network requires width $\Theta(\varepsilon^{-n})$ at constant depth,
but width $\Theta(\varepsilon^{-1})$ per coordinate if depth may grow like $\log(1/\varepsilon)$,
with an explicit depth--width exchange constant; the range bound $[1,e]$ pins the modulus of
continuity feeding a Jackson-type estimate.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[necessity of $\exp$/$\log$ for constant-depth high-degree representation]
No EML term of depth $< 3$ represents $x^n$ on $(0,\infty)$ for $n \ge 2$, and depth $3$ is
achievable only by using at least one $\exp$ and one $\log$; constant-depth representation of all
monomials forces both transcendental primitives.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

On the unit cube, a single nonlinearity replicated across coordinates yields a universally dense
algebra --- density flowing from injectivity of $\exp$ via Stone--Weierstrass --- and the cube
supplies the explicit shallow range $[1,e]$. For the monomial family, $\exp$ and $\log$ compress
representation depth from $n$ to a constant $3$ with an unbounded gap. Together they delineate
the two faces of expressive power: a soft qualitative reach and a sharp quantitative cost, with
the exponential and logarithm cast simultaneously as the universal builder and the great
compressor.

